european defence policy will be a  highlight as EU leaders meet in Brussels  this week looking to bolster the union's  military capabilities and boost the  defense industry the council where  defense is being discussed for the first  time in five years will urge countries  to deepened defense cooperation and  improve Europe's capacity to conduct  operations abroad the aim is to increase  the effectiveness and visibility of  security and defence policy and  strengthen the defense industry while  developing more efficient military  capabilities the industry totals some  400,000 jobs and creates almost a  million more in other areas helping to  increase competitiveness is likely to  boost employment further as well as  economic growth turning to the economy  the leaders are likely to call for  economic policies to be better  coordinated to encourage higher levels  of sustainable growth and help detect  vulnerabilities at an early stage  they're expected to underline the need  to advance reforms in areas central to  encouraging growth and job creation the  talks could also turn to the banking  Union and ambitious new safety mechanism  to ensure that EU member states will be  able to weather the financial crises of  the future in international affairs the  leaders will also discuss developments  in Ukraine following the government's  decision to postpone signing a major  agreement on closer ties and a trade  pact thousands of people have been  protesting daily in Kiev since the  decision last month the council will  also address the issue of illegal  immigration and particularly the plight  of would-be asylum seekers crossing the  Mediterranean in poorly maintained boats
 

